	"Do not meddle in the affairs of wizards," they say, "for you are
	crunchy, and tasty with catsup."  Well, be that as it may, I don't
	know what the wizards on the Dev Team are planning for the future of
	NetHack, but I've got some ideas I wanna throw out for discussion.

	Many people have stated that they'd like to see a multi-player version
	of the game.  Well, that's all well and good, but have any of these
	people considered the monumental changes required to get to point B
	from our current point A?  I think what we need to think about is an
	orderly evolution to that point, and I propose a blueprint for such a
	progression.

	The first stage, call it version 4.0, would be a multi-_character_
	game, a la the Bard's Tale series, or a host of other computer games.
	Developing this sort of program would not be too difficult,
	considering the current state of the code.

	In a multi-character game, there are a host of possibilities:  an
	expanded quest, that might require a certain combination of character
	types and/or alignments; spells could be combined to create
	spectacularly good (or bad) effects; parties of characters could
	continue to play, even if several members were to be killed.  Of
	course, given the increased firepower of a party of characters, more
	powerful monsters would need to be added.  In addition, multiple views
	of the dungeons could be introduced at this stage, with the party
	traveling along as a single ascii character, but a nice zoom-in would
	take place when something interesting happened (battle, a
	sink/fountain, the Castle Drawbridge, etc.).

	Okay, that's version 4.0.  So now version 4.1:  this would introduce a
	measure of independent action among the members of the party.
	Several characters could split off, say, to examine the Gnomish mines,
	while the remaining ones could go off to hunt down Medusa, for
	example.  Characters within the party could interact with the other
	members, to the benefit of the individual character or the party at
	large.  New spells for healing would be a good thing, or simply
	variations on the existing ones (Cast which spell? "extra healing"
	Cast on which character? "...").

	This version would be tougher to play than version 4.0, since the more 
	powerful monsters introduced in that version would still be around 
	to plague the smaller parties.  And again, this game would likely last 
	longer than the current average game.

	Now, we're ready for version 5.0.  With the code already existing
	which supports the independent action of members of a party, and with
	the party mentality now running (amuck?) among the people who are
	playing the game, it would be a simple matter (relatively speaking, of
	course) to break out independent user interfaces for individual
	members of a party.  At this stage, there is still the nastiness of
	adding the support of play across networks, but it'd be a _much_
	smaller bridge to cross that it is now.

	Okay.  I know there are holes in this plan, big enough to fit a
	Jabberwock through.  It's just a concept.  But no-one else seems to be
	proposing any way of actually getting to that much-vaunted
	multi-player version of the game.

	Now, have at it!
